    "narrative": "Today\u2019s slate for **Ghost Ride the WHIP** is anchored by a high-upside outfield trio, with **Mickey Moniak** (75.98) and **Seiya Suzuki** (75.03) leading our efficiency metrics. **Mickey Moniak** is our top play, benefiting from a massive 14% park boost and a favorable matchup against a starter with a 4.85 xERA, while **Seiya Suzuki** capitalizes on his elite placement in the #2 hole against a struggling southpaw. Similarly, **Tyler Soderstrom** (72.72) slots in as a high-value start; his ability to exploit Lowder\u2019s 4.74 xERA and his solid batting order position make him an essential engine for our daily offensive output. Even against Mize, **Corbin Carroll** (66.43) remains a must-start due to his sprint speed and the premium we place on the stolen base category, though we\u2019ve docked him slightly for the catcher\u2019s arm talent.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Cole Young** (36.38), who narrowly missed the starting cut. While his position at the top of the order provided a 10% boost, the underlying skill of his opponent, Anderson, suppressed his scoring floor below our strict 40-point threshold, forcing us to favor the power-hitting profile of **Josh Naylor** (46.99). Meanwhile, we are closely monitoring the 33% rain risk for **Austin Riley** (51.86). Despite the weather concerns and his drop to the #8 spot, **Austin Riley** stays in our lineup because of his potential against a middle-of-the-road starter; we will be ready to pivot to our bench depth if the situation deteriorates before first pitch. We continue to manage our remaining roster assets\u2014including **Trevor Story**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Max Muncy**, **Isaac Paredes**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Edwin Arroyo**, and **Henry Bolte**\u2014to ensure we remain competitive in our category targets while keeping an eye on the health of **James Wood** and the status of **Jose Caballero**.",
